# Build artifacts. ⛔ NEVER COMMIT THESE.
#
# `build/klug` and `build/klug_agnos` were TRACKED until 0.1.7. agnos removed the same shape from its
# own tree at 1.56.44 and wrote down why: `tests/gpu/build/edgeasm` was committed at 108,784 B, and
# running it exited 95 printing "B4 PASS -- the tool reproduces a shipped iron-proven shader
# byte-for-byte", while `edgeasm.cyr` beside it COULD NOT COMPILE AT ALL. Anyone who verified the
# oracle by running the binary got a green light from a fossil.
#
# ⇒ A committed build artifact is not evidence about the source beside it. It is evidence about
# whatever source existed when someone last ran a compiler — and unlike a stale doc it PASSES,
# because a stale oracle does not fail, it agrees.
#
# This repo had a live instance of exactly that risk: at 0.1.7 the vendored `lib/result.cyr` was the
# v5.8.28 heap-form `Result` under a 6.6.0 `io.cyr`, while `build/klug_agnos` sat tracked and green.
/build/

# ⚠ CONSEQUENCE, RECORDED SO IT IS NOT A SURPRISE: agnos `scripts/burn/stage-tools.sh:129` stages
# this repo (`stage_one klug src/main.cyr klug`) as a SIBLING row, and sibling rows do NOT auto-build
# — only `agnos/*` in-tree rows do. So on a fresh clone with no `build/`, stage-tools now FAILS with
#     ERROR: .../build/klug_agnos not present — run with --build
# That is the intended trade: an actionable error beats a fossil that stages silently and burns to
# iron. Run `stage-tools.sh --build`, or `cyrius build --agnos src/main.cyr build/klug_agnos` here.

# ⚠ `lib/` IS TRACKED ON PURPOSE and must stay that way — it is the VENDORED stdlib snapshot, pinned
# by `[deps].stdlib` in cyrius.cyml and refreshed with `cyrius lib sync`. It is source, not output.

# cyrius dep-resolver lock file — `cyrius deps` writes it by default; nothing here reads it.
cyrius.lock
